      "math_academy_id": "e-12744",
      "problem": "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ For all $n \\in \\mathbb{N},$ $n$ is a composite number.",
      "worked_solution": "If we denote\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14257569/assets/6b623801f26e595ef6cc34036800888a5a3479798e354dde2547f61a94d1773d.svg)\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, P(n).\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\neg \\left( \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, P(n)  \\right) \\equiv \\exists n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\neg P(n).\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\exists n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\neg P(n)$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ **There exists** $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that $n$ is **not** a composite number.",
